# Create kibana_sdp_amdb as a lookup-mode index ON THE prod CLUSTER.
#
# Required only for the LOOKUP JOIN rule (10008_single_stage). On 9.3 a remote
# LOOKUP JOIN resolves the lookup index on each cluster it queries and joins
# against that cluster's own copy -- joining against the local copy while the
# data is remote ("coordinator mode") is 9.6+. Since the query reads
# prod:metricbeat-*, the copy has to live on prod.
#
# Run this against the prod cluster, NOT the local/CCS cluster. The local
# kibana_sdp_amdb stays exactly as it is; Stage 2 keeps using it until cutover.
#
# ##########################################################################
# THE COPY MUST CONTAIN DATA. An empty index is worse than a missing one.
# ##########################################################################
#
# Creating the index is only half the job. LOOKUP JOIN is a LEFT join, and every
# data row in this query comes from prod, so every join is evaluated against
# PROD's copy. The local copy is never consulted -- no local index appears in
# the FROM, so the local cluster contributes no rows to join.
#
# If prod's copy exists but is empty:
#   - every metric row matches nothing,
#   - group and alert_status come back null,
#   - "WHERE group == "10008" AND alert_status == "enabled"" drops every row
#     (null comparisons are not true),
#   - the rule runs GREEN and produces ZERO alerts, with no error anywhere.
#
# That is the dangerous failure: a missing index gets you a loud error (the one
# the editor is already showing), an empty one gets you silence. Populate it,
# then confirm with the Test query / _query calls in DIAGNOSTICS that rows come
# back BEFORE enabling the rule.
#
# It needs the same documents as the local index -- one per host, carrying at
# minimum hostname, group and alert_status. It does not need the other ~45 AMDB
# fields, and it does not need to be in the same shape as anything else on prod.
#
# Mapping below is only the subset the rule actually reads. A lookup index does
# not need the other ~45 fields of the full AMDB config, and keeping it narrow
# means less to replicate and no chance of a field from this index shadowing a
# metricbeat column during the join. If you would rather mirror the index
# wholesale (e.g. via CCR), that also works -- the join only requires that
# index.mode is lookup and that hostname, group and alert_status are present.
#
# Types are copied from the local index's mapping so the join key stays
# compatible: hostname is text with a .keyword subfield, which is why the rule
# joins ON hostname.keyword and not ON hostname.

PUT kibana_sdp_amdb
{
  "settings": {
    "index.mode": "lookup"
  },
  "mappings": {
    "properties": {
      "hostname": {
        "type": "text",
        "fields": {
          "keyword": {
            "type": "keyword",
            "ignore_above": 256
          }
        }
      },
      "group": {
        "type": "text",
        "fields": {
          "keyword": {
            "type": "keyword",
            "ignore_above": 256
          }
        }
      },
      "alert_status": {
        "type": "text",
        "fields": {
          "keyword": {
            "type": "keyword",
            "ignore_above": 256
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}

# Verify (on prod). index.mode must read "lookup"; a lookup index is always
# single-sharded.

GET kibana_sdp_amdb/_settings?filter_path=**.mode,**.number_of_shards

# And verify it is POPULATED (this is the check people skip). Must be non-zero:

GET kibana_sdp_amdb/_count

# Spot-check that the hostnames actually match what metricbeat reports, since a
# join on a mismatched hostname convention (short name vs FQDN, case) silently
# produces the same zero-alert result as an empty index:

GET kibana_sdp_amdb/_search
{
  "size": 5,
  "_source": ["hostname", "group", "alert_status"]
}

# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keeping it in sync
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The AMDB config changes rarely, so a periodic refresh is enough. Options, in
# rough order of least-moving-parts:
#
# 1. Reindex from remote, run on prod, pulling from the local cluster. Needs
#    reindex.remote.whitelist to include the local cluster's host on prod, and
#    the local host must be reachable from prod:
#
#      POST _reindex
#      {
#        "source": {
#          "remote": { "host": "https://<local-cluster-host>:9243",
#                      "username": "<user>", "password": "<pass>" },
#          "index": "kibana_sdp_amdb",
#          "_source": ["hostname", "group", "alert_status"]
#        },
#        "dest": { "index": "kibana_sdp_amdb", "op_type": "index" }
#      }
#
#    Schedule it (Watcher is gone here, so an external scheduler or the same
#    pipeline that maintains AMDB). Deletions need handling -- either recreate
#    the index each run, or reconcile.
#
# 2. Dual-write from whatever already populates the local kibana_sdp_amdb.
#    Cleanest if that process is under your control, since it removes the
#    replication step entirely.
#
# 3. Cross-cluster replication. Attractive because it is continuous and this
#    deployment already uses CCR, but note the direction: CCR pulls, so prod
#    would be the follower and would need the LOCAL cluster configured as a
#    remote on prod. The existing CCS link runs the other way (local -> prod),
#    so this needs a new remote-cluster connection that may not be there today.
#    Also confirm the follower keeps index.mode: lookup -- the join fails
#    without it -- and remember follower indices are read-only.
#
# Whichever you pick: if this index is missing, empty or not in lookup mode on
# prod, remote clusters default to skip_unavailable: true and "does not have the
# requested index" is a skip condition, so the rule runs GREEN with ZERO alerts
# rather than erroring. Always confirm with Test query in the rule editor after
# a sync change.
